  {
    "abstract": "Increasing urbanization has shifted urban land use from expansion to conversion. However, the limited research restricts understanding of urban development. This paper presents a model following the rent gap theory and multinomial logistic regression to reveal the capital maximization mechanism behind land use conversions. Taking New York City (NYC) as the test bed, the model simulates conversions among public, residential, commercial, industrial, and transportation land for 2014–2017 and 2017–2020, with validation accuracy rates of 84.62% and 80.39%, and Kappa coefficients of 0.80 and 0.63. Key findings include: (1) capital preference for short-cycle, high-return development drives public land conversion into residential and transportation land; (2) as capital accumulation potential in Manhattan becomes saturated, the frontier of capital accumulation has shifted towards the Bronx, Queens, and Staten Island, accelerating residential growth; (3) land conversion favours private profitability over public value, intensifying spatial inequality. These spatial patterns represent the institutionalized logic of capital accumulation in land use. The study calls for reorienting NYC’s urban development from capital efficiency to public value through mechanisms such as expanding Transfer of Development Rights (TDR), enhancing Privately Owned Public Spaces (POPS), strengthening ULURP, and establishing a public value assessment framework for spatial justice and sustainability.",
